(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-09"] = {"phy-12-09-m01":{"idea":"Ohm's law says current is proportional to voltage when the temperature is constant.","steps":["V = I R with R constant.","A graph of V against I is a straight line through the origin.","So the potential difference is proportional to the current."],"others":"Current is not independent of voltage. Voltage is not inversely proportional to current. R does not change with current in an ohmic conductor.","trap":"Diodes and filament lamps do not obey Ohm's law.","answer":"the potential difference across it is proportional to the current through it"},"phy-12-09-m02":{"idea":"The unit of resistivity comes from R = ρL/A.","steps":["ρ = R A/L.","Units: Ω × m²/m = Ω m.","So resistivity is in ohm metre."],"others":"Ohm is the unit of resistance. Siemens metre would be conductivity. Ohm per metre would be resistance per length.","trap":"Resistivity is a material property, in Ω m.","answer":"ohm metre (Ω m)"},"phy-12-09-m03":{"idea":"A longer wire resists more and a thicker wire resists less.","steps":["R grows with length L.","R falls with area A.","So R = ρ L/A."],"others":"ρA/L has the wrong dependence. ρLA grows with thickness. L/(ρA) uses 1/ρ.","trap":"Length on top, area below.","answer":"ρL/A"},"phy-12-09-m04":{"idea":"When a wire is stretched, its volume stays fixed, so its area shrinks as its length grows.","steps":["Volume L × A is constant.","Length 2L means the area is A/2.","R' = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4ρL/A = 4R."],"others":"Unchanged and twice ignore the shrinking area. Half is the wrong direction.","trap":"Stretching by a factor n multiplies R by n².","answer":"four times"},"phy-12-09-m05":{"idea":"Resistivity belongs to the material, not to the shape.","steps":["ρ depends on the material.","It also depends on the temperature.","It does not depend on the length or thickness of a given wire."],"others":"The other options make ρ depend on shape or leave out the material or the temperature.","trap":"Resistance depends on shape; resistivity does not.","answer":"the material and the temperature, but not on its length or thickness"},"phy-12-09-m06":{"idea":"Current equals charge density times area times drift speed.","steps":["I = n A e v_d.","Rearrange: v_d = I/(n A e).","That is the drift velocity."],"others":"InAe multiplies instead of dividing. I/(nA) leaves out the charge e. nAe/I turns it upside down.","trap":"Drift speed is tiny, about 0.1 mm/s.","answer":"I/(nAe)"},"phy-12-09-m07":{"idea":"A metal resists more when it is hot.","steps":["In a metal the number of free electrons barely changes with temperature.","The ions vibrate more, so electrons collide more often.","So the resistance increases."],"others":"Vibrating less happens when cooled. A constant resistance ignores the collisions. More free electrons is what happens in a semiconductor.","trap":"Metals: R rises with T. Semiconductors: R falls.","answer":"increases, because the ions vibrate more and collide with the electrons more often"},"phy-12-09-m08":{"idea":"Resistors in series carry the same current and their voltages add.","steps":["Same current I through both.","Voltages add: I R₁ + I R₂ = I R.","So R = R₁ + R₂."],"others":"1/R₁ + 1/R₂ is the reciprocal in parallel. R₁R₂ and R₁R₂/(R₁ + R₂) are parallel-type forms.","trap":"Series: add. Parallel: add the reciprocals.","answer":"R₁ + R₂"},"phy-12-09-m09":{"idea":"Two equal resistors in parallel give half of one.","steps":["1/R_eq = 1/R + 1/R = 2/R.","So R_eq = R/2."],"others":"R² and 2R do not match. R/4 would need four resistors.","trap":"Parallel is always smaller than the smallest.","answer":"R/2"},"phy-12-09-m10":{"idea":"A cell loses some voltage inside itself when current flows.","steps":["The internal resistance drops Ir.","Terminal voltage = emf − Ir.","So V = E − Ir."],"others":"E + Ir applies to charging. E/r and Ir do not match the voltage across the terminals.","trap":"Discharging: minus. Charging: plus.","answer":"V = E − Ir"},"phy-12-09-m11":{"idea":"The terminal voltage is above the emf only when current is forced backwards into the cell.","steps":["When charging, V = E + Ir.","That is bigger than E.","During discharge V = E − Ir, and in open circuit V = E."],"others":"Open circuit gives V = E exactly. Zero internal resistance gives V = E. A large load current lowers V.","trap":"Charging is the only time V is above E.","answer":"the cell is being charged by a current in the opposite direction"},"phy-12-09-m12":{"idea":"Kirchhoff's junction rule says charge is conserved at a junction.","steps":["In steady flow charge cannot pile up at a point.","So current in equals current out.","This is conservation of charge."],"others":"Gravitation, energy and momentum do not give the junction rule.","trap":"Junction rule: charge. Loop rule: energy.","answer":"conservation of charge"},"phy-12-09-m13":{"idea":"Kirchhoff's loop rule is energy conservation around a closed path.","steps":["A charge going round a loop returns to its start.","Its potential energy is the same as before.","So the sum of emfs equals the sum of potential drops."],"others":"Angular momentum, charge and mass conservation do not give the loop rule.","trap":"Loop rule: energy.","answer":"conservation of energy"},"phy-12-09-m14":{"idea":"A balanced Wheatstone bridge has no galvanometer current, because two points are at the same potential.","steps":["With no current in the galvanometer, the two junctions have equal potential.","The resistance ratios on both sides are then equal.","So P/Q = R/S."],"others":"PQ = R + S and P + Q = R + S do not follow. P/Q = S/R swaps R and S.","trap":"Match the ratio in the same arm order.","answer":"P/Q = R/S"},"phy-12-09-m15":{"idea":"In a metre bridge the wire resistance is proportional to its length.","steps":["X is on the left, so its wire length is l.","S is on the right, so its wire length is 100 − l.","X/S = l/(100 − l), so X = S l/(100 − l)."],"others":"S(100 − l)/l puts X on the wrong side. S × 100/l and S × l/100 do not use both wire lengths.","trap":"Unknown on the left, so l goes on top.","answer":"S × l/(100 − l)"},"phy-12-09-m16":{"idea":"A potentiometer measures emf because it draws no current at balance.","steps":["At the null point no current flows through the cell.","So there is no Ir drop inside the cell.","The reading equals the true emf."],"others":"A voltmeter gives the terminal voltage. A potentiometer does not have low resistance. It does not need a higher voltage than the cell.","trap":"Null point means zero current from the test cell.","answer":"at balance it draws no current from the cell"},"phy-12-09-m17":{"idea":"Check each form of the power formula by units.","steps":["P = V I, P = I²R and P = V²/R are all correct.","V²R has units of V² Ω, which is not watts.","So V²R is the form that is not power."],"others":"V²/R, I²R and VI are all correct expressions for power.","trap":"Voltage squared is divided by R, not multiplied.","answer":"V²R"},"phy-12-09-m18":{"idea":"Rated power and voltage give the working resistance of a bulb.","steps":["P = V²/R, so R = V²/P.","R = 220²/100 = 48 400/100.","R = 484 Ω."],"others":"111 Ω, 550 Ω and 455 Ω come from wrong formulas, like V/P or P/V.","trap":"Use the rated voltage squared.","answer":"484 Ω"},"phy-12-09-m19":{"idea":"Joule heating is current squared times resistance times time.","steps":["Power in a resistor is I²R.","Energy over a time t is power × t.","So heat = I² R t."],"others":"I R² t has the squares on the wrong quantity. I²R/t divides by time. IRt is not heat.","trap":"Current is squared.","answer":"I²Rt"},"phy-12-09-m20":{"idea":"The kilowatt hour is a unit of energy.","steps":["1 kW h = 1000 W × 3600 s.","= 3.6 × 10⁶ J.","So it is energy, not power or charge."],"others":"It is not power, because it has a time in it. It is not a charge unit. 3.6 × 10³ J is off by a factor of 1000.","trap":"kW is power; kW h is energy.","answer":"energy, equal to 3.6 × 10⁶ J"},"phy-12-09-m21":{"idea":"Resistor colour code: first two bands are digits, the third is the power of ten.","steps":["Brown = 1 and black = 0, so the digits are 10.","Red as multiplier = 10².","Value = 10 × 100 = 1000 Ω. Gold means ±5%."],"others":"102 Ω reads red as a digit. 10 Ω ignores the multiplier. 1200 Ω comes from a wrong digit.","trap":"Third band is the multiplier.","answer":"1000 Ω (1 kΩ), with ±5% tolerance"},"phy-12-09-m22":{"idea":"Standard resistors need a resistance that does not drift with temperature.","steps":["Manganin and constantan have a very small temperature coefficient.","So their resistance hardly changes when they warm.","That is why standard resistors use them."],"others":"A large resistivity alone is not the reason. A negative coefficient would also drift. Zero resistivity would be a superconductor.","trap":"Stable value, not a big value.","answer":"a very small temperature coefficient of resistance"},"phy-12-09-m23":{"idea":"An ammeter measures the circuit current, so it is in series. A voltmeter measures across a part, so it is in parallel.","steps":["The ammeter must carry the whole current: put it in series.","The voltmeter measures the voltage between two points: put it in parallel with the part.","That matches the third option."],"others":"Both in parallel would short the ammeter. Both in series would block the circuit with the voltmeter. The reverse swap would give wrong readings.","trap":"Ammeter: in series. Voltmeter: in parallel.","answer":"the ammeter in series and the voltmeter in parallel with the part measured"},"phy-12-09-m24":{"idea":"A good voltmeter has a very high resistance.","steps":["It is connected in parallel with the part being measured.","A high resistance draws almost no current.","So it hardly disturbs the circuit."],"others":"A resistance equal to the circuit would disturb it. Zero resistance would short the part. A very low resistance would draw a large current.","trap":"Ideal voltmeter: infinite resistance. Ideal ammeter: zero.","answer":"a very high resistance, so that it draws almost no current"},"phy-12-09-m25":{"idea":"Drift velocity is proportional to current when n, A and e are fixed.","steps":["I = n A e v_d.","n, A and e do not change.","So doubling I doubles v_d."],"others":"Not unchanged. Not four times, because there is no square. Not halved.","trap":"Linear, not squared.","answer":"doubles"},"phy-12-09-m26":{"idea":"Identical cells in parallel keep the emf but share the internal resistance.","steps":["In parallel, the emf stays E.","The internal resistances are in parallel: r/n.","So the equivalent is E and r/n."],"others":"nE and nr is the series result. nE and r/n and E and nr mix the two cases.","trap":"Series adds emf. Parallel keeps emf.","answer":"E and r/n"},"phy-12-09-m27":{"idea":"With no current, there is no voltage drop inside the cell.","steps":["Open circuit: I = 0.","The internal drop Ir = 0.","So the terminal voltage equals the emf."],"others":"It is not greater than the emf. It is not zero. Less than E by Ir is true only when a current flows.","trap":"Emf is the open-circuit voltage.","answer":"equal to its emf"},"phy-12-09-m28":{"idea":"A shunt in parallel with a galvanometer turns it into an ammeter.","steps":["Most of the current should bypass the coil, so the shunt is in parallel and small.","Same voltage across both: I_g G = (I − I_g) S.","So S = I_g G/(I − I_g)."],"others":"A series resistor would raise the resistance, which is a voltmeter. The other parallel form has the factors in the wrong places.","trap":"Ammeter: shunt in parallel, small.","answer":"in parallel with the galvanometer, with S = I_g G/(I − I_g)"},"phy-12-09-m29":{"idea":"A large resistor in series with a galvanometer turns it into a voltmeter.","steps":["At full scale the voltage V is shared by G and R in series.","V = I_g (G + R).","So R = V/I_g − G."],"others":"Parallel forms would lower the resistance. V/I_g + G adds G wrongly. I_g G/V is not a series resistance.","trap":"Voltmeter: big resistor in series.","answer":"in series with the galvanometer, with R = V/I_g − G"},"phy-12-09-m30":{"idea":"Current sensitivity is how much the pointer turns per unit current.","steps":["Current sensitivity = θ/I.","More deflection for the same current means a more sensitive instrument.","Voltage sensitivity is θ/V."],"others":"The current for full scale is the opposite idea. The potential difference for full scale and deflection per volt are voltage versions.","trap":"Sensitivity is deflection per unit, not the unit per full scale.","answer":"the deflection produced per unit current"},"phy-12-09-m31":{"idea":"A uniform wire with a steady current drops voltage evenly along its length.","steps":["The voltage drop is V = I × (resistance of the part).","Resistance of a part is proportional to its length.","So the voltage between two points is proportional to the length between them."],"others":"Inverse, squared or independent relationships do not match a uniform wire.","trap":"Potentiometer wire: V ∝ l.","answer":"proportional to the length of wire between them"},"phy-12-09-m32":{"idea":"At balance, the emf is proportional to the balancing length.","steps":["Each emf equals the potential drop along its balancing length.","The gradient is the same for both cells.","So E₁/E₂ = l₁/l₂."],"others":"A squared ratio, an inverted ratio and a difference of lengths do not follow from V ∝ l.","trap":"Ratio of emfs equals ratio of lengths.","answer":"E₁/E₂ = l₁/l₂"},"phy-12-09-m33":{"idea":"A smaller potential gradient makes the potentiometer more sensitive.","steps":["A smaller gradient means volts per metre is lower.","A given emf then balances at a longer length.","A longer length can be read more precisely."],"others":"A driver cell with lower internal resistance does not set the gradient by itself. A shorter wire with the same current raises the gradient. Increasing the gradient makes it less sensitive.","trap":"Lower gradient, higher sensitivity.","answer":"decreasing the potential gradient along the wire"},"phy-12-09-n01":{"idea":"Ohm's law gives the current from voltage and resistance.","steps":["I = V/R.","V = 20 V and R = 10 Ω.","I = 2.0 A."],"trap":"Current = voltage ÷ resistance.","answer":"2.0 A"},"phy-12-09-n02":{"idea":"Resistance of a wire is R = ρL/A.","steps":["ρ = 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ Ω m, L = 100 m, A = 1 mm² = 10⁻⁶ m².","R = 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ × 100/10⁻⁶.","R = 1.7 Ω."],"trap":"Convert mm² to m² (10⁻⁶).","answer":"1.70 Ω"},"phy-12-09-n03":{"idea":"For two resistors in parallel, use product over sum.","steps":["1/R = 1/12 + 1/4 = 1/12 + 3/12 = 4/12.","R = 12/4 = 3 Ω.","Or product over sum: 48/16 = 3 Ω."],"trap":"The answer must be less than 4 Ω.","answer":"3.0 Ω"},"phy-12-09-n04":{"idea":"Find the current first, then the terminal voltage.","steps":["I = E/(R + r) = 12/(5.5 + 0.5) = 2 A.","V = E − I r = 12 − 2 × 0.5.","V = 11.0 V (same as I R = 2 × 5.5)."],"trap":"Terminal voltage is below the emf when current flows.","answer":"11.0 V"},"phy-12-09-n05":{"idea":"Power in a resistor is V²/R.","steps":["V = 220 V and R = 484 Ω.","V² = 48 400.","P = 48 400/484 = 100 W."],"trap":"This is the resistance of a 100 W bulb.","answer":"100 W"},"phy-12-09-n06":{"idea":"Energy in kilowatt hours = power in kW × time in hours.","steps":["Time = 90 minutes = 1.5 h.","E = 2 kW × 1.5 h.","E = 3.0 kW h."],"trap":"Change minutes to hours.","answer":"3.0 kW h"},"phy-12-09-n07":{"idea":"Drift speed comes from I = n A e v_d.","steps":["v_d = I/(n A e) with A = 10⁻⁶ m².","n A e = 8.5 × 10²⁸ × 10⁻⁶ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 13 600.","v_d = 2/13 600 = 1.47 × 10⁻⁴ m/s = 0.147 mm/s."],"trap":"Drift speed is much slower than you might expect.","answer":"0.147 mm/s"},"phy-12-09-n08":{"idea":"A balanced Wheatstone bridge has P/Q = R/S.","steps":["S = R Q/P.","R = 15, Q = 20, P = 10.","S = 15 × 20/10 = 30.0 Ω."],"trap":"Check the order in P/Q = R/S.","answer":"30.0 Ω"},"phy-12-09-n09":{"idea":"Metre bridge: X/S = l/(100 − l).","steps":["l = 40 cm, so 100 − l = 60 cm.","X = S × 40/60 = 10 × 2/3.","X = 6.67 Ω."],"trap":"The unknown is on the left, so it is the smaller one when l is below 50.","answer":"6.67 Ω"},"phy-12-09-n10":{"idea":"Cells in series add emf and internal resistance.","steps":["Total emf = 4 × 1.5 = 6 V.","Total resistance = 5.2 + 4 × 0.2 = 6.0 Ω.","I = 6/6 = 1.0 A."],"trap":"Include all the internal resistances in the total.","answer":"1.0 A"},"phy-12-09-n11":{"idea":"Shunt value from S = I_g G/(I − I_g).","steps":["I_g = 0.001 A, G = 50 Ω, I = 1 A.","S = 0.001 × 50/(1 − 0.001) = 0.05/0.999.","S = 0.0501 Ω, about 0.050 Ω."],"trap":"The shunt is tiny, and connected in parallel.","answer":"0.050 Ω"},"phy-12-09-n12":{"idea":"Series resistance for a voltmeter: R = V/I_g − G.","steps":["V/I_g = 10/0.001 = 10 000 Ω.","Subtract the galvanometer resistance: 10 000 − 50.","R = 9950 Ω."],"trap":"Do not forget to subtract G.","answer":"9950 Ω"},"phy-12-09-n13":{"idea":"On a potentiometer, emfs are in the ratio of balance lengths.","steps":["E₂/E₁ = l₂/l₁.","E₂ = 1.02 × 75/51.","E₂ = 1.50 V."],"trap":"75/51 is about 1.47.","answer":"1.50 V"},"phy-12-09-n14":{"idea":"Internal resistance by the potentiometer method: r = R (l₁ − l₂)/l₂.","steps":["l₁ = 80 cm (open circuit) and l₂ = 60 cm (with the resistor).","r = 4 × (80 − 60)/60.","r = 4 × 20/60 = 1.33 Ω."],"trap":"l₁ is the balance with no load.","answer":"1.33 Ω"},"phy-12-09-n15":{"idea":"Kirchhoff's loop rule with opposing cells.","steps":["Opposing polarities: the net emf is 12 − 6 = 6 V.","The loop rule: 12 − 6 − I × 3 = 0.","I = 6/3 = 2.0 A."],"trap":"Opposing emfs subtract.","answer":"2.0 A"}};
